-- WASM benchmark fixture: naive recursive fib(32).
--
-- The corpus had no CALL-BOUND module. Every other fixture is a tight
-- arithmetic loop, which the JIT compiles and which is latency-bound on its
-- own dependency chain -- so every runtime lands within 20% of every other and
-- the table says nothing. This one separates them:
--
--   duo compile bench/fib.duo --target wasm32-wasi -o bench/fib.wasm
--
-- It also pins the engine's largest known gap. the engine's ARM64 JIT implements `call`
-- by INLINING the callee (src/engine.duo, `elseif op == 16`), capped at depth 6,
-- and a bail returns -1 for the WHOLE body. Recursion can never be inlined
-- away, so this module always falls back to the interpreter and runs ~11x
-- slower than wasmtime. Any future real-call support in the JIT should show up
-- here first; until then the row is the honest cost of the inline-only design.
--
-- fib(32) = 2178309, verified against wasmtime.

fib(k: i32): i32
    if k < 2
        return k
    end
    fib(k - 1) + fib(k - 2)
end

-- GAP-12: `@c.export` does not attach to a value-form binding, and it binds to
-- the NEXT bare declaration -- so it must sit directly above `run`, not above
-- `fib`. Placing it above `fib` exports the wrong arity and the C build fails.
@c.export("run")
run(): i32
    fib(32)
end

main = (): i32
    run()
end
